diff --git a/HEGEL_REPORT.md b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..bc9d9c5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `pathfinding` 4.15.0
+
+`hegeltest` 0.28.2 added as a dev-dependency (`cargo add --dev hegeltest`, no
+MSRV friction: crate pins 1.88.0). 11 hegel tests covering 10 distinct
+properties were added to five existing integration-test files
+(`tests/pathfinding.rs`, `tests/topological_sort.rs`,
+`tests/strongly_connected_components.rs`, `tests/connected-components.rs`,
+`tests/kuhn_munkres.rs`). All tests generate small random graphs (adjacency
+lists over ≤ 8–10 nodes, non-negative weights mixing a tiny range — for ties
+and zero-cost edges — with values up to `u32::MAX`).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| # | Property | Test | Oracle / evidence |
+|---|----------|------|-------------------|
+| 1 | `dijkstra`, `astar` (zero heuristic) and `fringe` all return the optimal cost, and `None` exactly when the goal is unreachable | `pathfinding.rs::hegel_props::dijkstra_astar_fringe_agree_with_bellman_ford_oracle` | Hand-written Bellman–Ford relaxation oracle; cross-implementation agreement (three independent impls of the same query) |
+| 2 | `idastar` agrees with `dijkstra` on cost and reachability | `pathfinding.rs::hegel_props::idastar_agrees_with_dijkstra` | Cross-implementation agreement. Graphs capped at 6 nodes / weight ≤ 8: IDA* re-explores simple paths once per cost bound, exponential on dense cyclic graphs — a test-runtime bound, not a contract bound |
+| 3 | `dijkstra`'s returned path is a real witness: starts at start, ends at goal, consecutive nodes joined by edges, cheapest-edge costs sum to the returned cost | `pathfinding.rs::hegel_props::dijkstra_path_is_a_valid_witness` | Structural check against the generated adjacency lists |
+| 4 | `astar` with a *consistent* heuristic (exact distance-to-goal computed on the reversed graph) still returns the optimal cost | `pathfinding.rs::hegel_props::astar_with_consistent_heuristic_finds_optimal_cost` | Bellman–Ford oracle both for the heuristic and the expected cost; doc contract "the heuristic must not be greater than the real cost" |
+| 5 | `bfs` returns a valid path with the minimum number of hops, `None` iff unreachable | `pathfinding.rs::hegel_props::bfs_returns_a_valid_minimum_hop_path` | Hand-written queue BFS distance oracle; doc contract "the shortest path is returned" |
+| 6 | `dijkstra_all` returns exactly the reachable nodes (excluding `start`, documented) with optimal costs, and every parent is joined to its child by a real edge | `pathfinding.rs::hegel_props::dijkstra_all_matches_oracle_distances` | Bellman–Ford oracle; exercises the separate `dijkstra_partial` code path |
+| 7 | `topological_sort` returns every node exactly once with all edges pointing forward, or reports a node that genuinely lies on a cycle | `topological_sort.rs::hegel_props::topological_sort_orders_edges_or_reports_a_cycle_node` | Structural order check; hand-written reachability search for the cycle claim. Generator forces a DAG (edges oriented along a random permutation) half the time so the `Ok` branch fires reliably |
+| 8 | `strongly_connected_components` partitions the nodes so that two nodes share a component iff they are mutually reachable | `strongly_connected_components.rs::hegel_props::sccs_partition_nodes_by_mutual_reachability` | Floyd–Warshall boolean reachability closure |
+| 9 | `components` merges groups into exactly the partition computed by union-find | `connected-components.rs::hegel_props::components_agree_with_union_find` | Hand-written union-find oracle; node values mix a tiny range (frequent cross-group collisions) with full `u8` |
+| 10 | `kuhn_munkres` / `kuhn_munkres_min` return a valid assignment (one distinct column per row, weights summing to the returned total) equal to the brute-force max/min over all assignments | `kuhn_munkres.rs::hegel_props::kuhn_munkres_matches_brute_force_maximum`, `::kuhn_munkres_min_matches_brute_force_minimum` | Exhaustive enumeration of all column permutations (rows ≤ 4, columns ≤ 5). Weights bounded to ±1,000,000: sum overflow is documented to panic and is outside the tested domain |
+
+Generator notes: duplicate edges and self-loops are deliberately allowed
+(properties 1–8); weights include 0 (dijkstra/astar/fringe/idastar accept
+zero-cost edges); start == goal is generated (trivial one-node paths).
+
+## Test results
+
+- Full suite green: `cargo test` — all unit, integration, and doc tests pass,
+  including the 11 new hegel tests at the default 100 cases.
+- Liveness verified: every one of the 10 properties was temporarily mutated
+  (broken oracle `du + w + 1`, inverted assertions, off-by-one hop counts) and
+  every mutation was caught with a minimal shrunk counterexample (e.g.
+  `adj = [[], [(0, 0)]]`, `groups = []`, `adj = [[0]]`).
+- Guarded branches confirmed non-vacuous: the `Some(path)` branches of
+  properties 3 and 5, and *both* the `Ok` and `Err` branches of property 7,
+  were each shown to fire by mutating the assertion inside the branch and
+  observing a failure.
+- Exploratory 10× run: all 11 tests run with `test_cases = 1000` — green.
+  Committed tests use the default count.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. All algorithms agreed with each other and with the independent
+oracles on every generated input, including zero-weight edges, duplicate
+edges, self-loops, unreachable goals, start == goal, and weights up to
+`u32::MAX`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright. Two properties were deliberately shaped by
+documented contracts rather than weakened after failures:
+
+- `dijkstra_all` was expected *not* to contain `start` even when `start` lies
+  on a cycle — the doc says "not including `start`" — and the test asserts
+  its absence rather than a cycle-cost for it.
+- The astar heuristic uses a large finite stand-in (`1 << 60`) for "infinite"
+  distance on nodes that cannot reach the goal, because `cost + h` is
+  computed inside astar and `u64::MAX` would overflow (a test-arithmetic
+  concern, per the skill's wrapping-arithmetic guidance).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Oracle Sourcing section mapped cleanly onto this crate: the
+  "multiple implementations of the same query" pattern (dijkstra / astar /
+  fringe / idastar) is the crate's dominant oracle and found the API surface
+  easy to cross-validate.
+- A note on *algorithmic* cost of the code under test would help: IDA* is
+  correct but exponentially slow on dense cyclic graphs with many distinct
+  path costs, so the generator had to bound node count and weight range for
+  that one property. This is the runtime analog of the "bound protects the
+  test's resources, not the library's contract" guidance, which currently
+  only mentions memory/overflow.
+- `hegel::one_of!` between two differently-configured generators of the same
+  concrete type worked without `.boxed()`, as the reference promises.
+- The reference's warning about unused `Generator` imports was accurate:
+  none of these tests use combinators, and importing the trait produced
+  warnings until dropped.
